  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    **Six hours until the western shelf drops**
    
    Floe-master Rescus~ found them in the memorial chamber, breathing hard, one flipper pressed to his throat in the gesture every tunnel-worker made when speaking of time running out. "The western shelf. The vents—" He stopped. Nightshelf didn't waste words on what everyone already understood.
    
    The Pta∴extinct. Again. Or still — maybe it had never stopped, just slowed to a frequency below hearing.
    
    Three fire-cores left in the sling, and the ice beneath Sleek☽ Caemomot was singing.
    
    Augur ☽'Milucaum~ pressed a flipper to the tunnel wall and counted. One pulse. Two. The third came faster than the second — the intervals had been even when they'd checked at the western staging point two hours ago. Saermaev~ crouched behind them with the supply crate strapped across her back, nineteen fire-cores packed in charcoal-cloth, the last shipment that would ever move through this route if the engineers' estimates held. Six hours. That's what Crypt-Vendor Motlaeca-oss~ had said, her voice flat as quarry-stone: six hours before the western shelf drops and takes every tunnel between here and the eastern depots with it.
    
    That had been two hours ago. Augur had spent one finding the entrance. The main approach had collapsed — orca shore-breakers hammering the surface ice, the percussive rhythm translating through eighty body-lengths of stone and crystal into a fine white dust that coated everything. They'd found a secondary shaft, too narrow for the crate, and Saermaev~ had unloaded the fire-cores into three separate slings while ☽'spectus held a guttering core overhead and swore quietly about the footing. Four hours left. Call it three, because engineers lied in the optimistic direction when they wanted to seem brave.
    
    At Augur's hip, Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~ hung in its sheath, volcanic glass and shadow-steel and a weight that never felt the same twice. Their scarred flippers knew the grip like a burn scar knows its source. They did not touch it. Across their back, alongside one of the fire-core slings, ☽'Ilsancuia Depth-walker's ice-pick dragged at their shoulder with every step — memorial weight, Signum∴men weight, the kind of weight you don't set down because setting it down means the dead don't matter, and the dead always matter, and the dead are heavier than the living.
    
    Three hours to move through two leagues of corrupted trading post. Simple math. Impossible math.
    
    "Move," Augur said, and didn't wait.
    
    They descended.
    
    ---
    
    **Three hours, thirteen minutes remaining**
    
    The corruption seep had swallowed the northern passage. Augur smelled it before the fungi-light revealed it: mineral-stink and deep-pressure wrongness, the scent of ice remembering things that hadn't happened yet. The wall wept bioluminescent blue in slow rivulets, and where the rivulets pooled, the stone had gone translucent — you could see through it to the fissure-light below, pulsing like a heartbeat that predated everything alive on the Berg.
    
    "Shortcut." ☽'spectus was pointing with his good flipper — the left one, the one without the deep-tissue frost scarring. "Through the whisper-zone. Forty body-lengths. I've done it."
    
    The whispers were already audible. Faint. Almost words. Augur caught fragments — *third channel, western breach, six pod-lengths from* — and clamped down on the instinct to listen. The whisper-echoes near the fissures said things that sounded like tactical intelligence, and some of it was accurate, and that was the trap. The blade at their hip hummed. It wanted proximity to the fissures. Augur knew why.
    
    They could not say why.
    
    The Pactum∴silenti sat in their throat like a stone. What the blade fed when drawn near the crystallized fissures. What the Pta∴extinct had cracked open. What lived in the frequency beneath the singing ice. The words were right there — *don't go near the fissure because the blade will* — and they died before Augur's beak could shape them. Not law. Gag.
    
    "Long way," Augur said.
    
    "Long way costs an hour."
    
    "Long way."
    
    ☽'spectus stared. Saermaev~ shifted the crate-weight on her back, said nothing. The cold-burn discoloration on her left flipper had spread to the wrist since the last forced stop — there were no rests on this run. The corruption was accumulating faster in the young ones.
    
    They took the long way. Forty-seven minutes through a collapsed trading gallery where the Icebound Exchange's crude expansion tunnels intersected Shadow Coven architecture. The ceiling sagged. ☽'spectus's fire-core guttered twice, and both times the darkness rushed in like water, and both times Augur's flipper went to the blade's hilt and stopped. Drew back. The tremor in the flippers was worse now — corruption exposure or exhaustion or both; the body didn't distinguish.
    
    They turned left at the third body. Tormin, the foreman's apprentice, still frozen in the posture of someone reaching for a wall that wasn't there anymore. ☽'Ilsancuia's pick bit into Augur's shoulder, and they thought of Kaela keeping Tormin's ice-pick unused, unsharpened, hung like an accusation. Memorial weight recognizing memorial weight.
    
    ---
    
    **One hour, forty-one minutes remaining**
    
    The hunt-song hit Augur's sternum before the sound reached their ears. Low. Bone-deep. The whole tunnel hummed with it, the walls vibrating at frequencies that made the fungi-light stutter and the fire-core in ☽'spectus's grip flare bright and then nearly die. Orcas. Below? Above? Somewhere in the ice, and the ice carried their song so perfectly that distance was meaningless — they could be six body-lengths away or six hundred.
    
    "Floor," Saermaev~ said. Her first word in an hour.
    
    Augur looked down. The stone beneath them was crystallized — Pta∴extinct aftermath, the geometric impossibility of Crypt-seal☽ Teneecloris~ extending its influence through the substrate. The crystal pulsed with light that belonged to no aurora and no fire-core, and through it, Augur could see void. Empty space beneath them, vast and dark, and somewhere in that dark, something that glowed.
    
    "Spread weight," Augur started, and ☽'spectus fell through.
    
    Not all the way. His rear flippers punched through the crystallized floor to the hip, and the ice screamed — not metaphor, actual sound, a frequency that harmonized with the hunt-song and made Augur's vision go white at the edges. ☽'spectus was wedged, bleeding, crystal edges biting into him, and the fire-core dropped from his grip and fell into the void below and fell and fell and did not land.
    
    Darkness. Only the fungi-light remained: sick blue, pulsing like a wound.
    
    Augur drew Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~.
    
    The remaining light died. Every fungal glow within thirty body-lengths snuffed as if the blade had inhaled the photons. Saermaev~ made a small sound. ☽'spectus swore. Augur was already cutting — by feel, by the memory in their scarred flippers, volcanic glass meeting crystal and the crystal exhaling rather than shattering. The sound of wounds that exhale. No crack, no break, just a slow release of breath from stone that had been holding it since the Pta∴extinct.
    
    The blade pulled. Toward the fissure below. Toward whatever pulsed in the void. It wanted to go down, and Augur's wrist ached with the effort of keeping the cuts lateral, freeing ☽'spectus instead of following the blade's hunger into the deep where something watched through crystallized light.
    
    The edge caught Augur's left flipper on the last stroke. Drew blood.
    
    The wound went silent.
    
    Not numb — *silent*. Like the injury had happened to someone else, somewhere else, in a moment the ice decided not to remember. Blood ran down Augur's flipper without sound, without pain, without anything but the knowledge that the blade had taken something and given nothing back. They kept cutting.
    
    ☽'spectus came loose. Augur sheathed the blade. The fungi-light crept back, reluctant, dimmer than before. Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~ was warm against their hip. Warmer than it should be. Blood dripped from their left flipper in absolute silence, and Saermaev~ stared at it — at the way it hit the stone and made no sound — and looked away fast.
    
    "Drop the pick," ☽'spectus said, limping, blood on the ice behind him. "Augur. Drop ☽'Ilsancuia's pick. You can't carry everything."
    
    Augur shifted the memorial weight on their shoulder. The ice-pick's haft was worn smooth by hands that would never hold anything again. You don't drop a Signum∴men relic. You don't. Even when you should.
    
    "Move," Augur said.
    
    ---
    
    **Seventeen minutes remaining**
    
    The eastern tunnel mouth was visible — fifty body-lengths, maybe sixty, a pale glow of functioning fire-core braziers on the far side where the corruption hadn't reached. Behind them the ice groaned, a sound so deep it was felt rather than heard, the western shelf beginning its long collapse. Under their flippers the floor cracked in hairline fractures that raced ahead of each step.
    
    Saermaev~ was stumbling. The cold-burn discoloration had reached her neck, frost-patterns on her feathers that meant the corruption was in her blood now; she'd need a cleansing ritual within hours or the whispers would start making sense to her, and after that, the Touched never came all the way back. ☽'spectus dragged his injured leg and said nothing, because there was nothing to say at this speed.
    
    Thirty body-lengths. The ceiling shed crystal dust. Behind them, a section of tunnel folded inward with a sound like the Berg exhaling, and the air that rushed past them was cold — not fire-core cold, not winter cold, but the deep wrong cold of corruption surging through newly opened fissures.
    
    Something crystalline bloomed across the tunnel ahead. A corruption barrier — the Pta∴extinct's legacy, crystal and light-that-shouldn't-exist, sealing the last twenty body-lengths.
    
    No time to go around. No time to think.
    
    Augur drew Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~ and the world went dark and they cut. By feel. By the ache in their forging-scarred flippers, by the pull of ☽'Ilsancuia's pick on their shoulder, by the sound of wounds that exhale as the blade opened the crystal wall breath by breath. The blade sang. The fissures below sang back. Augur's wrist burned where the Radiant Ring pressed dull against their skin, counterweight to the blade's hunger, and somewhere in the mineral dark Saermaev~ was breathing too fast and ☽'spectus was shoving them both forward through the gap Augur carved. Blood from Augur's silent wound painted the crystal edges and made no sound at all — not drip, not splash, not anything — and the cut stone swallowed it like offering.
    
    Light. Fire-core light, real light, warm and orange and honest.
    
    They fell through. The barrier re-crystallized behind them, and behind that, the tunnel collapsed, and behind that, the western shelf dropped into the deep with a sound that went on longer than seemed possible.
    
    ---
    
    The fire-cores were intact. Nineteen in Saermaev~'s slings, distributed immediately to the hands that rushed forward from the eastern depot. Enough to keep the braziers burning for another week. Not enough. Never enough.
    
    Rescus~ was waiting at the depot's edge, fresh fire-cores on his belt and a question he didn't ask. He looked at Augur's left flipper — at the wound that bled without crying out, at the silence where pain should be — and touched his ancestor-bone clasp. Said nothing.
    
    Augur knelt on stone that didn't pulse, didn't hum, didn't remember. Veil☽ Sasvaeumis~ steamed in its sheath, darker than when they'd started. ☽'Ilsancuia's ice-pick pressed into the floor beside them, upright, because they would not lay it down. The tremor in their flippers had spread to their whole body — fine, constant, the kind of shaking that doesn't stop when the danger does. Their feathers bore cold-burn patches along both flippers. The Radiant Ring sat dull on their right flipper, still warm from its long argument with the blade.
    
    Saermaev~ sat against the depot wall, eyes closed, fungi-frost on her neck glowing faintly in the brazier-light. ☽'spectus was letting someone bind his leg and staring back at the sealed tunnel where the supply route used to be.
    
    Pactum∴silenti intact. Signum∴men fulfilled. The pick still upright. The blade still dark.
    
    Augur touched the ancestor-bone clasp at their throat. Held it. Let go.
    
    Their left flipper bled without making a sound, the wound holding secrets it would never speak. Somewhere far below, the ice remembered faster than anyone could run.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771587856990_0", anchor: "orca shore-breakers hammering the surface ice", text: "Shore-breakers create access channels. Herders drive prey toward killing zones. Finishers wait in the water below escape routes. Hunt-songs coordinate at bone-aching frequencies. This chronicle captures two of these — shore-breakers and hunt-songs — and treats both as atmosphere. They are not atmosphere. If shore-breakers were working the surface above a known supply route, the full tactical suite was almost certainly in motion. These three were inside a killing zone and the chronicler either did not know or chose the more dramatic framing of nature-as-obstacle over the more terrifying truth of coordinated predation.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771587856990_1", anchor: "nineteen fire-cores packed in charcoal-cloth, the last shipment", text: "Three penguins carrying fire-cores through a sealed tunnel under mortal risk, operating between settlements, functionally inviolable because the cargo matters more than they do. The Courier Corps carries civilization in sealed crates on their backs, abandons birth-names for trade-names, marks their flippers with ink that glows in both light and dark. These three have no trade-names, no ink, no formal Accord protections. They are doing the work. The institution either had not formed yet or had not reached them. I find I cannot decide which is sadder.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771587856990_2", anchor: "The Pactum∴silenti sat in their throat like a stone", text: "The administrative ice preserves the Pactum as information-control legislation — routing tables, custody chains, clearance tiers. Bureaucratic architecture. Nothing in the three drafts I found suggests a mechanism for physical enforcement of silence. Either the chronicler is dramatizing, which would be forgivable, or the Pactum operates at a level the administrative record does not capture. The Nightshelf have always been better at recording what they do than explaining how they do it.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587856990_3", anchor: "third channel, western breach, six pod-lengths from", text: "Pod-lengths. Orca unit of measurement. The whisper-echoes are replaying orca tactical communication preserved in the ice near the fissures — residue, not fabrication. Accurate, outdated, and therefore more dangerous than lies.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587856990_4", anchor: "the Icebound Exchange's crude expansion tunnels intersected Shadow Coven architecture", text: "Two construction eras in a single corridor. The Exchange expansion tunnels post-date the Flipper Accord's first signing — commercial infrastructure built when surface-deep trade formalized. The Shadow Coven passages are older, almost certainly Faction Wars-era, when coven workshops clustered near geothermal seeps for forge access. The chronicler walks through both without marking the transition. Not error — the tunnel itself is layered this way. But the atmosphere of ancient dread the passage borrows belongs to the older stratum. The Incursion-era tunnel is just badly built.", type: :temporal, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587856990_5", anchor: "engineers lied in the optimistic direction when they wanted to seem brave", text: "The Foundation Depths contain fourteen structural assessments from the Incursion period. Every one overestimated time-to-failure. Augur's correction factor — assume two-thirds of stated time — matches the actual collapse data so precisely I sat back from this page and stared at the wall of my empty archive for several minutes.", type: :tangent, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771587856990_6", anchor: "the wound holding secrets it would never speak", text: "I wrote, in a previous annotation on this blade, that it was forged to suppress every communicative impulse. Here is the proof walking: a wound that bleeds without sound, pain that arrives without signal, injury the ice itself declined to record. The maker built silence into the steel. The chronicler has — perhaps accidentally — shown what that means when the blade turns on its wielder.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
